The recurrent activities passed through a 20-unit fully connected layer and were mapped to two Q values (the expected reward of the selected action, given the current state):
(6)
ut=σ(Wfcht+bc)Qt=WQut+bQ,Qt∈R2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & u_{t}=\sigma\left(W_{f c} h_{t}+b_{c}\right) \\ & Q_{t}=W_{Q} u_{t}+b_{Q}, Q_{t} \in \mathbb{R}^{2}, \tag{6} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where Wfc ∈ ℝ20×100, bfc ∈ ℝ20, WQ ∈ ℝ2×20, and bQ ∈ ℝ2. Actions at ∈ {0, 1} were sampled from the Boltzmann distribution over Q values:
(7)
P(at=a∣st)=exp⁡(Q(st,a)/τ)∑a′exp⁡(Q(st,a′)/τ), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P\left(a_{t}=a \mid s_{t}\right)=\frac{\exp \left(Q\left(s_{t}, a\right) / \tau\right)}{\sum_{a^{\prime}} \exp \left(Q\left(s_{t}, a^{\prime}\right) / \tau\right)}, \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where temperature τ = 0.2. We used a simple per-trial Q-learning rule to improve interpretability and stability of learning compared to those of an actor-critic rule. At the end of trial t, the agent received the total reward. The instantaneous loss was
(8)
Lt=(Rt−Q(st,at))2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} L_{\mathrm{t}}=\left(R_{t}-Q\left(s_{t}, a_{t}\right)\right)^{2} . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Immediately after each trial, parameters θ were updated via plain SGD and TBPTT:
(9)
θ←θ−η∇θLt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \theta \leftarrow \theta-\eta \nabla_{\theta} L_{\mathrm{t}}, \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where η is the learning rate.

Given that our networks were trained using BPTT for three steps, we computed the chain Jacobian over three consecutive time steps within each episode. For each hidden state h, we computed the derivative of the softplus activation, which corresponded to the sigmoid function σ(z) = 1/(1 + e−z), and constructed the diagonal matrix D = diag(σ(z)). The local Jacobian was given by
(13)
J=DWrec, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} J=D W_{\text {rec }}, \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Wrec is the recurrent weight matrix. Because we updated the weights after every trial (three steps), we obtained the chain Jacobian by multiplying the local Jacobians over three time steps:
(14)
Jtotal=J3J2J1. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} J_{\text {total }}=J_{3} J_{2} J_{1} . \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Finally, we computed the spectral radius of the chain Jacobian, defined as the maximum absolute eigenvalue:
(15)
ρ=max(|λi|),λi∈eigvalsUtotal). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.\rho=\max \left(\left|\lambda_{i}\right|\right), \quad \lambda_{i} \in \text { eigvals } U_{\text {total }}\right) . \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This spectral radius served as an index of the network’s ability to retain input information.
